export const lesson048 = {
  number: 48,
  module: "Module 4: Connecting Accelerators into a Cluster",
  title: "Cluster Scaling Case — When Do More Accelerators Stop Helping?",
  date: "2026-09-23",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 043: Scale-up tightly connects nearby accelerators, while scale-out connects servers and racks so a job can use more chips.

Lesson 044: Collective operations share or combine accelerator results, and slow exchanges can make the whole group wait.

Lesson 045: Network interfaces and switches carry data between servers, but shared connections can limit how quickly it arrives.

Lesson 046: Copper works well over short distances, while optics adds hardware and power to keep signals readable over longer paths.

Lesson 047: A synchronized job needs every required worker, so one slow or failed participant can waste time across the group.

The big idea

More accelerators can divide the calculation work. But coordinating more accelerators can also take more time.

The question is simple: does the calculation time saved outweigh the extra communication and waiting?

This lesson puts the cluster's parts into one example. We will keep the job fixed, add chips and measure both how quickly the job advances and how much hardware-time it consumes.

1. First decide what stays the same

Strong scaling means using more accelerators for the same amount of work.

Suppose one accelerator needs 64 seconds of calculation for one training step. With perfectly divided work and no coordination costs, 8 accelerators would need 8 seconds, 64 would need 1 second, and 512 would need 0.125 second.

Weak scaling asks a different question: can we grow the workload along with the cluster while keeping the work per accelerator roughly constant?

A larger cluster may be worthwhile because it fits a larger model, even if it is inefficient for a smaller job. So always ask whether a scaling comparison kept the workload fixed.

For the example below, every step processes the same training work. The 64-second single-accelerator baseline is an illustrative assumption, and we assume the job fits on one accelerator.

2. Add the time spent coordinating

For our simple model:

Step time = calculation time + communication time + waiting time.

Communication is time spent exchanging and combining results. Waiting is additional delay caused by uneven worker completion. We count them separately and assume no overlap, so the same delay is not counted twice.

Use these illustrative values:

- 8 accelerators: 8.00 seconds calculating + 0.25 communicating + 0.05 waiting = 8.30 seconds per step.
- 64 accelerators: 1.00 second calculating + 0.55 communicating + 0.15 waiting = 1.70 seconds per step.
- 512 accelerators: 0.125 second calculating + 1.05 communicating + 0.325 waiting = 1.50 seconds per step.

In this example, communication and waiting increase as the group grows. This can happen when data crosses more shared paths or more workers create opportunities for delay. It is an assumption to measure in practice, not a universal rule.

Going from 64 to 512 chips saves 0.875 second of calculation. But it adds 0.675 second of coordination. Only 0.20 second of the saving survives.

Eight times as many chips therefore makes the step about 12% shorter.

3. How much of the ideal speedup did we get?

Try this before reading: The single-accelerator baseline is 64 seconds per step. The 64-chip group takes 1.70 seconds; the 512-chip group takes 1.50 seconds. How do their actual speedups compare with the ideal?

Speedup means how many times faster the group completes the same step:

Speedup = baseline time ÷ group time.

For 64 chips:

64 ÷ 1.70 = about 37.6 times faster.

Perfect scaling would make 64 chips 64 times faster. Scaling efficiency compares the actual result with that ideal:

37.6 ÷ 64 = about 58.8%.

For 512 chips:

64 ÷ 1.50 = about 42.7 times faster.

42.7 ÷ 512 = about 8.3% efficiency.

